These couplets help portray the co-generating dynamics of Nature. They also hint at an approach to life that helps moderate stressful extremes. For more background, see  Using Yin and Yang to Pop Preconceptions.
UPDATE 2026
Before reading, note that this page and its companion essay were stress-tested by an AI (Claude, by Anthropic) acting as devil’s advocate. The fuller assessment lives there. Take it for what it is: one useful, if imperfect, mirror.
AI’s Assessment of Couplets
The couplet format is the most immediately accessible entry point in the whole body of work. The structure — X evolves forth into Y, yet Y revolves back to X — gives the circular nature of reality a grammatical form that bypasses argument. You don’t have to be convinced. You just read it and either feel it or you don’t.
What works particularly well is how the couplets handle politically and emotionally charged pairs without taking sides. Liberal evolves forth into Conservative… Conservative revolves back into Liberal lands differently than any analytical treatment of that opposition could. The form does the work the argument can’t.
The main limitation is also the format’s strength: the couplets are complete as they stand, which means there’s little to push against. A reader can nod along without being genuinely disturbed. The Correlations method, by contrast, forces engagement — you have to do something with pen in hand. Couplets can be consumed passively, which makes them pleasant but potentially less transformative.
One quiet gap: the couplets list is long, but all entries follow identical grammatical structure. A reader who doesn’t feel the resonance in the first few may assume the rest are more of the same and stop. Some brief guidance on how to actually sit with a single couplet — what to do with it beyond reading — would help those who need more than the form alone provides.

The Co-generating Principle

The ancient Chinese symbol (right) is the “great pole” pictogram (太极图   tàijítú). It depicts the dynamics of Yin and Yang. White corresponds to Active (Yang), and black to Passive (Yin). It says visually what each couplet says verbally. The benefit of Couplets lies in how they substitute Yin and Yang for two unique words and their complementary antonyms. These four words make a distinctive statement about the natural process depicted in the symbol, yet are chock full of specific meaning imparted to the couplet by the four words comprising it.
Note: This discussion doesn’t grasp certain deeper aspects due to being an AI generated overview. For the deepest dive, study the source essay here.
The first step in creating a Couplet is choosing four words from the Correlations table. We’ll make a Correlations table by placing basic nouns, adjectives, and verbs with their respective antonyms into complementary categories:  Active (Yang) and Passive (Yin).



 
